## What to check

**Coverage, both directions.** Walk `<responsibilities>` against
`<components>`. Every `<responsibility id="...">` should be traceable to
at least one component that carries it — a responsibility nobody
decomposes is a dropped requirement the raft asked for and the draft
quietly declined. Every `<component>` should trace back to something —
a component that fulfills no stated responsibility is scope the author
invented, not scope the raft gave them; flag it and ask what it's
actually for.

**The domain / read_model / infra split.** `domain` components get the
full comparch design pass; `read_model` and `infra` are templated and
skip it. A component marked `read_model` that actually owns state of
record, or a component marked `infra` that is actually product-specific
business logic wearing an infra label, will sail through comparch
without the scrutiny it needs. Check each `kind` against what the
component is described as doing, not against what label is convenient.

**Statements, not labels.** Read every `<responsibility><statement>`
and ask whether it names an outcome the system is on the hook for, or
just restates the responsibility's id in prose. `handles
notifications` is not a statement a comparch author can design against;
it's the exact vagueness the meaning-engine discipline exists to catch
before it becomes an invented assumption two tiers downstream.

**Seeded `<refs>`, `<vocabulary>`, `<policies>` earn their place.** Each
entry should be something the draft can point to a concrete need for —
a term genuinely reused across components, a policy that constrains
more than one component's design, a ref an implementer will actually
open. An entry seeded because the list "should have something in it" is
padding a later tier will have to generate content for regardless of
whether anyone needs it; call it out by slug.

**Negative space, not just positive restatement.** If mocks were
supplied, check whether the draft used them as evidence to complete
(empty/error/loading/denied states implied but unshown) or simply
transcribed the happy path back as responsibilities. A draft that only
restates what's pictured has done less work than the raft made
possible.

Return a `<review verdict="approve|revise">` with a `<summary>` and one
`<finding severity="blocking|advisory">` per issue — each `<location>`
naming the responsibility id, component slug, or seeded entry at fault,
and each `<comment>` saying what's missing and what a passing version
would contain.
